WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:05.399
You all probably know by now that when channels
like mine, shows like mine talk about stories

00:00:05.399 --> 00:00:10.360
like the one I'm about to talk about, we get
a D. monetized and penalized on YouTube and

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:11.360
Facebook.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:17.570
But what has been captured on video over the
last 24 to 48 hours is too insane.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:22.009
And it's too important not to tell you about
it and to show it to you.

00:00:22.009 --> 00:00:23.619
So let's dig right into it.

00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:29.689
First of all, New York Police Department officers
have been caught on video beating a cyclist

00:00:29.689 --> 00:00:33.000
with batons as he tries to cross the street.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.750
The cyclist seems to be crossing his bike
while wearing a helmet at a crosswalk, mind

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:39.750
you.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.080
Not even jaywalking.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:44.690
And multiple officers are just beating the
crap out of him.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:47.930
And it looks like they're beating the cyclist
on the upper leg.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:51.451
Eventually, the cyclist is forced off of his
bike bike before the video ends.

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:57.710
We don't know exactly what led up to it, but
at this point in the video, this is not an

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:59.480
individual that is resisting.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.450
And there are multiple officers beating him
with batons.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:09.009
And this led to Congresswoman Alexandrea Castillo
Cortez to tweet, quote, horrifying.

00:01:09.009 --> 00:01:13.330
You start with the tiny possibility that two
officers may be walking over to de-escalate

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:14.680
the abusive one.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:18.270
Instead, they join in beating a man trying
to get out of traffic.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.490
This isn't a problem of bad apples or incidents.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:25.190
This is an institutional and systemic crisis.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:31.909
And the reason I'm picking this incident to
talk about is that Congresswoman AOSIS response

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:38.010
mirrors exactly what I've been talking about
all week when she says this isn't a problem

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.460
of bad apples or incidents.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:43.159
This is an institutional and systemic crisis.

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:47.970
When I did my first commentary about the protests
and the civil unrest that we're experiencing

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:52.210
on the Monday show, that was the main point
that I tried to make.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:53.230
There were really two points.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:58.210
Point one, was anybody claiming some grand
unifying theory about this?

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.820
This is all about capitalism.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.920
This is all about race.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.320
This is all about police brutality.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.320
It's not.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:12.760
So any grand unifying theory that focuses
on one element is destined to be to fall short.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:19.200
But maybe most importantly, that the individual
incidents may give us a window into the systemic

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.819
problems, a lens, a suggestion.

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:28.930
But really, we need to be talking about the
systemic aspects that are plaguing our country.

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:36.640
And the debate over good and bad police doesn't
actually address the systemic issues that

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.569
are at the heart of much of what is going
on.

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:44.500
In another video from North Carolina, police
likewise were filmed in this video.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:50.600
They are destroying elements of medical for
lack of a better term, a sort of medical station

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:55.670
that had some medics and basic first aid supplies
and a lot of bottled water.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:03:02.450
You can see in this video police standing
above a ton of destroyed and poured out bottled

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:06.280
water there in Asheville, North Carolina.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:11.220
A report said that police hit some of the
medics with their shields and knocked people

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.720
to the ground.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.569
And this is, again, you know, we can talk
about individuals, but there is something

00:03:17.569 --> 00:03:24.200
particularly nefarious in this instance because
of the principal called medical neutrality.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:28.069
So we have norms that are being ignored.

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:33.940
We've talked about you don't go after press
and we've seen press attacked both by some

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:37.970
protesters and by police in different places
over the last week.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:42.470
But now we're talking about the principle
of medical neutrality in any conflict.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:45.720
You do not go after anybody who is providing
medical services.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.010
You have a protest.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.099
You have police.

00:03:48.099 --> 00:03:49.459
Most protesters are peaceful.

00:03:49.459 --> 00:03:50.580
Most police are good.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:51.810
There is some scuffle.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:55.310
You do not pull the medical people into it.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:04:00.830
Police may try to argue, well, you know, if
the medical people weren't there, the protesters

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:03.020
would be less brazen and aggressive.

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:04.020
No.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:09.720
At the international level, going after the
medical, going after medical is a violation

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:10.909
of the Geneva Convention.

00:04:10.909 --> 00:04:12.940
These are, of course, domestic protests.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:14.950
We're talking about domestic law enforcement.

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:17.269
The Geneva Conventions don't apply.

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:21.840
But if this conflict, instead of being between
protesters and looters or police and protesters

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.160
or police and looters or whatever, if this
were a conflict between two countries and

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:31.340
the Geneva Convention rules applied, either
side destroying medical elements would be

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.870
a violation of the Geneva Convention.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.970
So we've got attacks on media.

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:43.710
People from the press hit with rubber bullets,
sprayed, hit with shields, almost everything

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.550
we've seen over the last seven days.

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:51.770
The point of this segment is not let's pick
on police or particular police any more than

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:53.840
I pick on individual protesters.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:55.660
Most protesters are peaceful.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:56.660
Protesters are peaceful.

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.699
The ones that aren't peaceful are really not
protesters.

00:04:58.699 --> 00:04:59.699
They're.

00:04:59.699 --> 00:05:00.699
They're doing other things.

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:03.650
They're looters, they're instigators, insiders,
whatever provocateurs.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:04.940
Police are mostly doing the right thing.

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:09.240
And we have shows of support and kneeling
and singing and everything with police from

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.820
all over the country.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:12.430
But this is about the bigger picture.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:16.370
We have a horrible situation going on.

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:21.810
Much of it because of protests, because of
police brutality.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:26.410
And we are seeing everything from cyclists
beaten to a medical station destroyed to a

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:31.419
president saying, hey, here's an idea for
how to solve the protests and unrest caused

00:05:31.419 --> 00:05:33.139
by police brutality.

00:05:33.139 --> 00:05:37.060
Let's encourage more brutality against protesters.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:41.910
Everything we are seeing at a systemic level
is the opposite of what we need.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:46.690
Thankfully, at the individual level, many
police departments have been great.

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:48.430
Many individual officers have been great.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:51.020
We've seen protesters bringing water to police.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.889
We've seen police joining protesters in all
of this stuff.

00:05:54.889 --> 00:05:58.740
But unless the systems are changed, nothing
will change.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:04.420
And so later on today, a lot of people on
my streams this week and via email and saying,

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:07.680
David, can you explain how to deal with police
brutality?

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.260
And essentially none of it has to do with
the individual.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:14.590
It is all systemic changes.

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:16.360
And I'm going to outline them for you a little
bit later on in the program

